        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How fast should you move a surface cleaner?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A common guideline is about one foot of travel every one to two seconds, and slower on heavily soiled or textured surfaces. The bar needs time to sweep each strip several times. If you can see a distinct clean and dirty arc next to each other while you work, you are moving too fast and should slow down."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much should surface cleaner passes overlap?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Overlap each pass by roughly one-third to one-half of the deck's width. For a 16-inch deck that means stepping over five to eight inches per lane. The outer edge of a spinning bar cleans less evenly than the centre, so overlap is what keeps the finished slab an even colour."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do you need to rinse after using a surface cleaner?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. A surface cleaner lifts dirt into a slurry but does not carry it away. Rinse the whole area downhill with a wide nozzle afterwards. Without a rinse, the dirty film dries back onto the concrete and often shows as faint swirls or a dull haze across the slab."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you use detergent with a surface cleaner?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You can, but most results come from pre-treating the surface rather than running soap through the deck. Apply detergent or degreaser through your washer's soap system or a pump sprayer, let it dwell for the time on the label, then clean with the surface cleaner. Flush the attachment with clean water afterwards."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will a surface cleaner work on an electric pressure washer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can, if the deck is sized for low flow. Electric washers typically supply less GPM than gas machines, so a 12 to 14 inch deck, or one sold as electric-compatible, suits them best. A large deck designed for gas machines will spin slowly on an electric washer and leave an uneven finish."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you use a surface cleaner on pavers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, with care. Keep the deck moving, avoid lingering over joints and consider a lower pressure. The jets can strip joint sand, which then needs replacing. Many owners re-sand with polymeric sand after cleaning. Natural stone pavers may need even gentler treatment than concrete pavers."
          }
        },
